ork / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Gwalior Fort is a hill fort above the city of Gwalior in Madhya Pradesh, India, one of the strongest fortresses of northern India.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gwallawg-ap-lleenog"><span class="enc-name">Gwallawg ap Lleenog</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gwallawg ap Lleenog was a 6th-century British king in northern Britain, possibly of Elmet.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gwandu-emirate"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gwandu-emirate.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gwandu Emirate</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gwandu Emirate was the western half of the Sokoto Caliphate, governed by Abdullahi dan Fodio and his descendants after the caliphate was divided between him and…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-stele"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gwanggaeto-stele.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gwanggaeto Stele</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gwanggaeto Stele is a granite monument about six metres tall erected in 414 by King Jangsu of Goguryeo at the old capital, present-day Ji'an in Jilin, China, in…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-the-great"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gwanggaeto-the-great.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gwanggaeto the Great</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gwanggaeto the Great was the nineteenth king of Goguryeo, reigning from 391 to 413.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gwanghaegun-of-joseon"><span class="enc-name">Gwanghaegun of Joseon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gwanghaegun was the fifteenth king of Joseon, ruling from 1608 to 1623.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gwangjong-of-goryeo"><span class="enc-name">Gwangjong of Goryeo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gwangjong was the fourth king of Goryeo, ruling from 949 to 975.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gwangmu-reform"><span class="enc-name">Gwangmu Reform</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gwangmu Reform was the modernisation programme of the Korean Empire, named after Emperor Gojong's era name Gwangmu, used from 1897 to 1907.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gwenwynwyn-ab-owain"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gwenwynwyn-ab-owain.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gwenwynwyn ab Owain</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gwenwynwyn ab Owain was prince of southern Powys from 1195, which was known after him as Powys Wenwynwyn.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gwgon-ap-meurig"><span class="enc-name">Gwgon ap Meurig</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gwgon ap Meurig was the last native king of Seisyllwg in south-west Wales.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gyanendra-of-nepal"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gyanendra-of-nepal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gyanendra of Nepal</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gyanendra was the last King of Nepal, reigning from 2001 to 2008.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gyebaek"><span class="enc-name">Gyebaek</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gyebaek was a general of the Korean kingdom of Baekje.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gyeon-hwon"><span class="enc-name">Gyeon Hwon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gyeon Hwon was a Silla soldier who rebelled in 892 and founded Later Baekje in south-western Korea, opening the Later Three Kingdoms period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gyeon-singeom"><span class="enc-name">Gyeon Singeom</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gyeon Singeom was the last king of Later Baekje in Korea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gyeongae-of-silla"><span class="enc-name">Gyeongae of Silla</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gyeongae was king of Silla from 924 to 927, during the Later Three Kingdoms period, when Silla had shrunk to a small state around its capital.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gyeongbokgung"><span class="enc-name">Gyeongbokgung</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Gyeongbokgung is the main royal palace of the Joseon dynasty in Seoul, built in 1395 after the capital moved to Hanyang.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gyeongdeok-of-silla"><span class="enc-name">Gyeongdeok of Silla</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gyeongdeok was king of Silla from 742 to 765, at the height of Unified Silla's culture.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gyeongguk-daejeon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gyeongguk-daejeon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gyeongguk daejeon</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gyeongguk daejeon, or National Code, was the basic law code of Joseon Korea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gyeongju.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gyeongju</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Gyeongju is a city in south-eastern South Korea that was the capital of Silla for nearly a thousand years, until 935.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gyeongsun-of-silla"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gyeongsun-of-silla.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gyeongsun of Silla</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gyeongsun was the last king of Silla, placed on the throne in 927 by the rebel leader Gyeon Hwon of Later Baekje after the sack of Gyeongju.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gyeongwon-yi-clan"><span class="enc-name">Gyeongwon Yi clan</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gyeongwon Yi clan, also known as the Incheon Yi, was one of the most powerful aristocratic lineages of the Korean kingdom of Goryeo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gyges-of-lydia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gyges-of-lydia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gyges of Lydia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gyges was king of Lydia from around 680 BCE and founder of the Mermnad dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gylippus"><span class="enc-name">Gylippus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gylippus was a Spartan general of the Peloponnesian War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium"><span class="enc-name">Gymnasium</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">A gymnasium was a Greek institution for physical training and education, which also served as a centre of social and intellectual life.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gympie"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gympie.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gympie</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Gympie is a town in south-east Queensland, Australia, on the Mary River.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gyo-dong-and-songhyeon-dong-tombs"><span class="enc-name">Gyo-dong and Songhyeon-dong tombs</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gyo-dong and Songhyeon-dong tombs are a group of large burial mounds in Changnyeong, South Korea, built mainly in the 5th and early 6th centuries by the elite of…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gyojeong-dogam"><span class="enc-name">Gyojeong Dogam</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gyojeong Dogam, or Directorate General for Policy Formulation, was the office through which the Choe military house ruled the Korean kingdom of Goryeo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gyorgy-dozsa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gyorgy-dozsa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">György Dózsa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">György Dózsa was a Székely soldier from Transylvania chosen to lead a crusade against the Ottomans proclaimed in Hungary in 1514.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gyptis"><span class="enc-name">Gyptis</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gyptis is the daughter of Nannus, king of the Segobrigii, in the foundation legend of Massalia (Marseille).</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gytheio"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gytheio.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gytheio</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Gytheio is a port